.TH REFS 1 "2026-10-07" "storage-spaces 1.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
refs \- read Microsoft ReFS volumes on Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B refs info
.I source
.br
.B refs ls
.I source
.RB [ \-\-path
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-R ]
.br
.B refs cat
.I source
.B \-\-path
.I file
.RB [ \-\-stream
.IR name " | " \-\-snapshot
.IR name ]
.br
.B refs stat
.I source
.B \-\-path
.I path
.br
.B refs mount
.I source mountpoint
.RB [ \-\-allow\-other ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B refs check
.I source
.RB [ \-\-quick ]
.br
.B refs set
.I device
.B \-\-path
.I file
.RB [ \-\-created | \-\-modified | \-\-changed | \-\-accessed
.IR time ]
.RB [ \-\-attributes
.IR bits ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.br
.B refs overwrite
.I device
.B \-\-path
.I file
.B \-\-at
.I offset
.B \-\-from
.I local-file
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.br
.B refs create
.I device
.B \-\-path
.I file
.RB [ \-\-from
.IR local-file ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.br
.B refs rename
.I device
.B \-\-path
.I file
.B \-\-to
.I name
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.br
.B refs delete
.I device
.B \-\-path
.I file
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.br
.B refs mkdir
.I device
.B \-\-path
.I directory
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.br
.B refs move
.I device
.B \-\-path
.I file
.B \-\-to
.I path
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.br
.B refs link
.I device
.B \-\-path
.I file
.B \-\-to
.I path
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.br
.B refs clone
.I device
.B \-\-path
.I file
.B \-\-to
.I path
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.br
.B refs write
.I device
.B \-\-path
.I file
.B \-\-from
.I local-file
.RB [ \-\-append ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.PP
where
.I source
is
.RB [ \-\-offset
.IR bytes ]
.I device
or
.B \-\-space
.I name disk ...
(options before the devices).
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B refs
reads volumes formatted with ReFS 3.x by Windows (Dev Drives, data
volumes, ReFS inside a Storage Spaces space): directories, files with
their data, sparse files, alternate data streams, hard links, symbolic
links and junctions, attributes and times. Only
.BR set ,
.BR overwrite ,
.BR write ,
.BR create ,
.BR rename ,
.BR move ,
.BR link ,
.B delete
and
.B mkdir
write to the volume.
.PP
Every metadata page is checked against the checksum its reference
records (CRC-64 or SHA-256, and CRC32-C for the superblock on 4 KiB
clusters); a page that fails is an error, not silently read. The data of
integrity streams is checked against its CRC32-C or CRC-64 on every read;
damaged data is an error (EIO in the mount).
.PP
The
.I device
is an image, a disk or a partition. On a disk the first ReFS partition of
its GPT is used, unless
.B \-\-offset
gives the volume's byte offset. With
.BR \-\-space ,
the devices are the member disks of a Storage Spaces pool and the volume is
read from the named space (the pool is read, not attached; see
.BR spaces (8)).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B info
The ReFS version, size, cluster size, serial number, metadata checksum,
the current checkpoint and the number of objects.
.TP
.B ls
List a directory (default the root):
.B \-l
adds attributes (d directory, l reparse point, r read-only, h hidden,
s system, a archive, S sparse), size and modification time (UTC);
.B \-R
recurses (links to directories are listed, not followed).
.TP
.B cat
Write a file's data, or with
.B \-\-stream
one of its named streams, or with
.B \-\-snapshot
one of its stream snapshots (made by
.BR "refsutil streamsnapshot" ),
to standard output.
.TP
.B stat
Everything about one file or directory: attributes, sizes, the four
times, where the record is, the data's extents, the named streams, the
stream snapshots and a link's target.
.TP
.B mount
Mount the volume read-only through FUSE, in the foreground (unmount with
.BR "fusermount \-u" ,
or
.B umount
as root).
Mounted by root, a block device is mounted as type fuseblk.refs, as
ntfs\-3g mounts are, which udisks2 counts as the device's mount:
.B refs
makes that mount itself, with the device's sector size as block size,
without fusermount3, and the mount goes away when
.B refs
ends, also when it is killed.
With
.B \-\-rw
(one image, disk or partition; not
.BR \-\-space )
the mount writes: files (written back when closed, the changed parts
in place as Windows changes them; the size limit is that of
.BR create ),
block clones (a whole file copied into an empty one with
copy_file_range),
directories, renames, hard links, times, the read-only attribute and
named streams, each change one transaction as the writing commands make
it. Experimental. The helper
.B mount.ReFS
(installed with
.BR refs )
mounts ReFS volumes for
.BR mount (8),
fstab and udisks2 this way, read-only unless the options hold both
.B rw
and
.BR refs.rw .
It returns once the mount is in place, or with the reason it is not;
opening a large volume on hard disks the first time can take minutes,
which it waits for (on a terminal it shows every 10 s how much has been
read; the environment variable
.B MOUNT_REFS_TIMEOUT
sets a limit in seconds).
Named streams appear as extended attributes
.BR user. \fIname\fR
(up to 64 KiB, the Linux limit; larger ones are read with
.BR "refs cat \-\-stream" );
symbolic links with an absolute target
.RB ( \e??\eX:\e\fIpath\fR )
point into the mount;
.B \-\-allow\-other
lets other users read it (needs
.B user_allow_other
in
.IR /etc/fuse.conf ).
Before it mounts, the quick checks run (see
.BR "check \-\-quick" ):
a volume that is not healthy is refused unless
.B \-\-force
is given, and is then read-only; a failed one is not mounted at all. For
a device of a space
.B spaces attach
attached (\fI/dev/mapper/ss\-*\fR), the verdict the space got counts too:
a space attached past its verdict mounts only with
.BR \-\-force .
.B mount.ReFS
passes the option
.B force
on as
.BR \-\-force ;
a refused mount says the first failing check and, as root, where the
report is
.RI ( /run/storage\-spaces/reports/refs\- serial .txt );
udisks2 answers "Unknown error when mounting" to whatever a mount helper
refuses, and keeps that line in its journal
.RB ( "journalctl \-u udisks2" ).
.TP
.B check
Check the volume's consistency without writing: every page's checksum
and structure (as Windows checks pages), every page and file cluster
used in its allocator, and clusters several files map counted in the
block reference count table. Exits with 1 when it finds a problem.
With
.B \-\-quick
only what mounting checks: the boot sector, the superblock and its copies
at the end of the volume, both checkpoints and the log (records newer
than the checkpoint mean Windows let go of the volume without dismounting
it: what refs reads is older than what Windows shows), and for
.B \-\-space
or a device of an attached space the checks of the pool and the space; a
report with the evidence and the verdict (healthy, degraded, suspect,
failed), exiting with 1 unless healthy.
.BR ls ,
.BR cat ,
.B stat
and
.B info
read a volume that is not healthy and say so on standard error; the
writing commands refuse it.
.TP
.B set
Change a file's times (UTC, "YYYY-MM-DD hh:mm:ss" or a FILETIME number)
or attributes (the bits users can set: read-only 1, hidden 2, system 4,
archive 0x20, ...), or with
.B \-\-integrity on
turn integrity streams on for an empty file. Experimental: only with
.BR \-\-yes ,
on a volume not attached anywhere else. Like every command that writes,
it writes the way Windows does (copy on write, then a new checkpoint),
so an interruption leaves the old volume.
.TP
.B overwrite
Replace bytes of a file with the content of
.I local-file
at byte
.IR offset ,
where they are (within the file's size; not in sparse ranges; the
clusters of an integrity stream it touches are copied on write with new
checksums, as Windows does; a file Windows compressed, up to 64 MiB, is
copied whole into ordinary clusters), and set its
modification and change times to now.
Experimental; only with
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
.B create
Create a file with the content of
.I local-file
(empty without), up to 64 GiB, named in printable ASCII,
read from it piece by piece. It takes the
permissions of the files beside it. Experimental; only with
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
.BR rename ", " delete
Rename a file or directory within its directory, or delete a file (one
name of a hard link; clusters other files share stay theirs), a named
stream
.RB ( \-\-stream ),
or an empty directory. Experimental; only with
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
.B mkdir
Create a directory. Experimental; only with
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
.BR move ", " link
Move a file or directory into another directory, or give a file another
name (a hard link). Experimental; only with
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
.B clone
Copy a file as a block clone, as
.B Copy\-Item
does on a Dev Drive: the copy (a new name, or an empty file) shares the
file's clusters, each counted once more in the block reference count
table; changing either copy later leaves the other as it was. The data
only (not named streams); small files are copied; both files must be
integrity streams or neither. Experimental; only with
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
.B write
Replace a file's content with that of
.IR local-file ,
or with
.B \-\-append
add it to the end; the file keeps its creation time, attributes,
permissions and named streams (as large as
.B create
takes, 2 GiB for integrity
streams; a file with stream snapshots changes in its live level, the
snapshots staying as they were; a file Windows compressed is written
whole into ordinary clusters). With
.BI \-\-stream " name"
it writes that named stream instead (small ones stay in the file's
record), and
.B delete \-\-stream
.I name
deletes one. Experimental; only with
.BR \-\-yes .
.PP
The writing commands refuse a volume whose log holds changes its last
checkpoint lacks (a volume detached without being dismounted;
.B refs info
shows it): attach it to Windows once and remove it safely first.
.SH LIMITS
Verified against Windows on ReFS 3.14 volumes (Windows 11) with 4 KiB and
64 KiB clusters, CRC-64 and SHA-256 metadata checksums and integrity
streams, and on ReFS 3.7, 3.4, 3.1 and 1.2 volumes (Windows Server 2022,
2019, 2016 and 2012 R2; 3.4 and 3.1 lay out file records differently, 1.2
also its pages). Other ReFS 3.x versions are read by the same
rules but were not verified. Only ReFS 3.14 volumes are written; the
writing commands and
.B mount \-\-rw
refuse others.
Encrypted files and ReFS 2.x are not read yet, nor the allocators of
ReFS 1.x by
.BR "refs check" ;
stream snapshots are read by
.B cat
but not shown in the mount.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
refs ls /dev/sdb2 \-lR
refs cat devdrive.img \-\-path /src/main.c > main.c
refs mount /dev/sdc mnt/
refs ls \-\-space Data /dev/sdd /dev/sde \-\-path /
refs mount \-\-space Data /dev/sdd /dev/sde mnt/
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR spaces (8),
.BR fusermount (1)
